#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Linear Function"}] :question/id #uuid "dc5cefa9-aa18-52d3-bbdc-a0c1050f6f3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14168" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 2x-1$ for $x \\in (-1,1].$ What is its range?" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/32/32925e498e936298ada23ba1c560b992b7dc2dce0e39fd6479bf6ba5ce2cb545.png)\n\nThen, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values on the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-3.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-3).$ However, the graph does not include this point.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs at the point $(1,1).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-3$ and $1,$ including $1$ but not including $-3.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-3 < y \\leq 1,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-3 < f(x) \\leq 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6df7104d-0f74-5301-9525-132c74c19ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Function by Considering Its End Behavior"}] :question/id #uuid "c639f820-2493-5267-9f59-9bb06e58dea7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14171" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown below?\n\n![](images/88/880925c00705275ef8630444a08053bc6ca2c79b2e4754a09d0bd7a2af059f32.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $-1.$ This occurs when $x\\to \\infty.$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-1$ and $\\infty,$ but not including $-1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-1 \\lt  y \\lt \\infty,$ which can also be written as  $-1 \\lt f(x) \\lt \\infty.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6727b20-2af5-5bff-aae4-e35c44fde144" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/id #uuid "1d74961e-433c-5c0f-8bdb-d279c5170cc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14173" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a277f71617ce2dceae7ee97017655c4c27e57a9bb22020995905760e53bf7720.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-2,1]$ is $[-2,2].$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (1,5)$ is $(1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-2,2] \\cup (1,3] = [-2,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179651 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6df7104d-0f74-5301-9525-132c74c19ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Function by Considering Its End Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61183e7c-37a9-558e-a0b0-83a789137f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty <f(x)\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507740} {:answer/id #uuid "68d47d85-f16f-5776-a976-e632cff15db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<f(x)<3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507752} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ced4ea-d105-56ab-8608-0c246f4ff01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507827} {:answer/id #uuid "cd16c9b5-3282-55e3-bf29-a47d56de1938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<f(x)<\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507882} {:answer/id #uuid "defaa840-22f8-5d9c-8600-eba8e5e96ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #e